          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "70"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.069,
            0.489,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.885,
            0.148
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "square is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "b ^ { 2 } ."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . The areas of the two rectangles are ab each. Together they make the bigger square; hence we can conclude that"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.396,
            0.155,
            0.638,
            0.174
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_2_equation_3.jpg",
          "content": "( a + b ) ^ { 2 } = a ^ { 2 } + 2 a b + b ^ { 2 } ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.183,
            0.885,
            0.222
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "From Fig. 4.2 it is clear that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "(a + b)^{2} = a^{2} + 2ab + b^{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for all a and b when a and b are lengths of line segments."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.222,
            0.886,
            0.281
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think of numbers a and b where a and b do not represent lengths of line segments. What if a and b are negative numbers? Let us check for some negative numbers and see if this equation still works."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.285,
            0.512,
            0.305
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 2:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Let "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a = - 2"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "b = - 3"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.31,
            0.514,
            0.33
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Then "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "(a + b) = -5   and   (a + b)^2 = 25."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.334,
            0.484,
            0.354
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Also "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a^{2}=4,b^{2}=9\\ and2ab=12."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.36,
            0.525,
            0.38
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Thus "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a^{2}+2ab+b^{2}=4+12+9=25."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.385,
            0.536,
            0.406
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Hence, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a^{2}+2ab+b^{2}=(a+b)^{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " again!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.409,
            0.861,
            0.451
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now suppose a and b are rational numbers, say "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a \\div \\frac { 2 } { 3 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "b = { \\frac { 3 } { 4 } }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.447,
            0.469,
            0.493
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Then "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "(a + b) = \\left( - \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{3}{4} \\right) = \\frac{1}{12}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.504,
            0.365,
            0.545
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_2_equation_13.jpg",
          "content": "( a + b ) ^ { 2 } = { \\frac { 1 } { 1 4 4 } }   ."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.553,
            0.769,
            0.66
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_2_equation_14.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}a^{2}+2ab+b^{2}=\\left(\\frac{-2}{3}\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)^{2}\\\\=\\frac{4}{9}-1+\\frac{9}{16}=\\frac{64-144+81}{144}=\\frac{145-144}{144}=\\frac{1}{144}\\end{aligned}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.672,
            0.885,
            0.732
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a^{2}+2ab+b^{2}=(a+b)^{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " seems to be true for rational numbers too. But we are still not sure if it is true for all numbers. To verify this, let us investigate further using the distributive property of numbers:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.739,
            0.618,
            0.782
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_2_equation_16.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{aligned} &(a+b)^{2}=(a+b)(a+b)=a(a+b)+b(a+b)\\\\ &=a^{2}+ab+ba+b^{2}=a^{2}+2ab+b^{2}.\\\\ \\end{aligned}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.787,
            0.885,
            0.827
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Recall that in Grade 8 you were introduced to "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "(a+b)^{2}=a^{2}+2ab+b^{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " as an identity."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.832,
            0.81,
            0.851
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What is the difference between an equation and an identity?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.851,
            0.887,
            0.91
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "An "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "algebraic identity",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is an equation that is true for all values of the variables occurring in it, while an equation need not be true for all values."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
